<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Władysław IV Vasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1595">1595</a>–<a href="/years/1648">1648</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Władysław IV · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-vasa">House of Vasa</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Władysław IV Vasa was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from 1632 to 1648. In his youth he was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> after Polish forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1610, though he never ruled there. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> describe this episode as a moment of overreach that showed the Commonwealth's offensive reach at its peak.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-iv-vasa.jpg" alt="Władysław IV Vasa" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Paul Rubens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rubens_Wladyslaw_Vasa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Władysław was born on 9 June 1595 at the royal summer residence of Łobzów, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a> and his first wife, Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. His father had inherited the Swedish throne in 1592 but was deposed in 1599 by his uncle, later Charles IX, which began a long feud in which the Commonwealth's Vasa kings claimed the Swedish crown.</p><p>His mother died in 1598, and he was raised by Urszula Meierin, a former lady of her court. He studied for several years at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-university">Kraków Academy</a> and for two years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and spoke and wrote German, Italian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a> the Seven Boyars elected the young Władysław tsar after the overthrow of Vasily Shuysky. His father, who aimed to convert <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to Roman Catholicism, refused the boyars' request to send him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and have him adopt Orthodoxy, and he never reigned. He mounted his own campaign in 1616, but could not take Moscow, and he kept the title of grand duke of Moscow without power until 1634.</p><p>In 1621 he was a commander at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khotyn">Chocim</a> in the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, where he urged the other commanders to stay and fight. In 1624 and 1625 he travelled in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> under the name Snopkowski, met Rubens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and was impressed by opera in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The election sejm of 1632 chose Władysław without serious rivals, and he was crowned in Wawel Cathedral on 6 February 1633. In the Smolensk War of 1632 to 1634 he broke the Russian siege of Smolensk in September 1633 and surrounded the army of Mikhail Shein, which surrendered on 1 March 1634. He modernised the army with Western infantry and artillery and tried to found a navy, which led to the port village of Władysławowo, but the ships were gone by the 1640s.</p><p>A Catholic, he was tolerant: the Senate had 6 Protestant members when he took power and 11 when he died. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szlachta">szlachta</a> thwarted his efforts to strengthen the monarchy, and he died on 20 May 1648 without a legitimate son. His half-brother John II Casimir Vasa succeeded him, and his reign was later remembered as a golden era of stability before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmelnytsky-uprising">Khmelnytsky Uprising</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">Deluge</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Władysław IV Vasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The high point of Commonwealth arms came in 1610, when Hetman Stanisław Żółkiewski defeated a larger Russian and Swedish force at the Battle of Klushino and Polish troops occupied Moscow, where a group of boyars elected Sigismund III’s son Władysław, the future <b>Władysław IV</b>, as Tsar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Łobzów, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Merkinė, in what is now <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.08,19.91&amp;year=1629" aria-label="Łobzów on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Władysław IV Vasa"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4449 5802h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4449 5802h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.08,19.91&amp;year=1629">What was here in 1629? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
